<% content_for :title, "Terms, privacy and abuse · Artifacto" %>

<main class="wrap legal">
  <p class="eyebrow"><%= link_to "← artifacto", root_path %></p>
  <h1>Terms, privacy and abuse</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Anyone can publish here without an account, so these rules are short and enforced
    by removal. Using the service means agreeing to them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">contact</h2>
  <% if @abuse_email %>
    <p>Abuse reports, takedown requests, legal notices and privacy requests:
      <a href="mailto:<%= @abuse_email %>"><%= @abuse_email %></a>.</p>
  <% end %>
  <p>Every artifact page also has a <strong>Report</strong> form in its <code>⋯</code> menu.</p>

  <h2 id="acceptable-use">acceptable use</h2>
  <p>Do not publish anything that is illegal where you or the server are, or that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sexualises minors, in any form;</li>
    <li>phishes, harvests credentials or payment details, or impersonates a person or organisation;</li>
    <li>delivers or links to malware, or attacks the people who open it;</li>
    <li>harasses, threatens or doxxes anyone, or incites hatred or violence;</li>
    <li>infringes someone else's copyright or trademark;</li>
    <li>is spam, or uses this service as general-purpose file or image hosting.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We remove anything that breaks these rules, block its bytes and every image embedded
    in it from being published again, and may refuse further uploads from the same
    source. We do not have to warn you first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takedown">reporting and takedown</h2>
  <p>
    Use the Report form on the artifact, or write to the address above with the artifact's
    link and why it should come down. Reports are read by a person, and content that
    breaks the rules above is removed as soon as it is confirmed. If you are the author
    and think something was removed by mistake, write to the same address.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">privacy</h2>
  <p>What we keep, and for how long:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Artifacts</strong> — the file, its title and settings. Deleted when the artifact
      expires: 1–30 days after it was published or last updated. Anyone with the link can read it,
      so do not publish secrets.</li>
    <li><strong>Edit tokens and PINs</strong> — stored only as hashes.</li>
    <li><strong>Uploader IP address</strong> — stored encrypted and erased 30 days after upload,
      so a lawful request about a specific artifact can be answered. A keyed hash of it is kept
      with the artifact to enforce bans.</li>
    <li><strong>Comments</strong> — the text, the optional name you typed, where on the page it
      was left, and a keyed hash of your IP address for moderation. Deleted with the artifact.</li>
    <li><strong>Reports</strong> — the reason, any details you gave, and a keyed hash of your IP address.</li>
    <li><strong>Server logs</strong> — record request IP addresses and are kept briefly for
      operating and securing the service.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking. The only cookie is a session cookie that
    protects forms and the admin sign-in. Your browser keeps the name you comment under, and
    an artifact's edit token if you enter one to delete comments, in its own local storage —
    never sent anywhere except with the request that needs it. Errors are reported to an
    error tracker without cookies, headers or request bodies. The service may sit behind a
    CDN such as Cloudflare, which sees requests in transit.
  </p>
  <p>To ask what we hold about you, or to have it deleted, write to the contact address above.</p>

  <h2 id="no-warranty">no warranty</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided as is, free, with no guarantee of availability, and artifacts
    disappear when they expire — keep your own copy of anything that matters. The software
    is open source under the MIT licence.
  </p>
</main>
